      <item>
         <title><![CDATA[Will Canadian workers refuse to handle Israeli goods? Why unions increasingly say they should]]></title>
         <link>https://www.cbc.ca/news/canada/clc-resolution-canadian-unions-israel-9.7428991?cmp=rss</link>
         <description><![CDATA[<img src='https://i.cbc.ca/ais/dda16fe4-5807-4956-8a97-66e5573eaf7a,1795128795489/full/max/0/default.jpg?im=Crop%2Crect%3D%280%2C613%2C8640%2C4860%29%3B' alt='People stand with a bombed-out building in the background' width='620' height='349' title='Palestinians inspect damage to a building and tents a day after they were hit by an Israeli airstrike in Gaza City, Wednesday, Nov. 11, 2026. (AP Photo/Jehad Alshrafi)'/><p>A movement to cut off Israeli goods is spreading across Canada's unions. The Canadian Labour Congress, which represents more than 3 million workers, passed a resolution last month declaring trade, services and relationships with Israel to be “hot cargo,"  emphasizing a call for workers  to refuse to handle weapons and military equipment destined for Israel.</p>]]></description>
         <pubDate>Sun, 22 Nov 2026 04:00:00 EST</pubDate>
         <category>News/Canada</category>
         <guid isPermaLink="false">9.7428991</guid>
         <dc:creator>Kevin Maimann</dc:creator>
